**Eczema** (also called **atopic eczema** or atopic dermatitis) is a long-term condition that makes skin **itchy, dry, cracked and sore**. It affects around **1 in 5 children** and **1 in 10 adults** in the UK. It often runs in families and is linked to asthma and hay fever.

## Symptoms

- **itchy**, dry skin
- red or darker patches, depending on skin tone
- cracked, sore or bleeding skin
- thickened skin from scratching

It often affects the hands, the insides of elbows, the backs of knees, and the face and scalp in children. Symptoms come and go, with **flare-ups**.

## Common triggers

- **soaps**, bubble bath and detergents
- **heat**, sweating and cold, dry weather
- wool and synthetic fabrics
- stress
- allergies to dust mites, pets or pollen
- infections

Food allergies trigger eczema in some babies and young children, but rarely in adults.

{% callout type="warn" title="Get medical help quickly" %}
See a GP the same day or call **NHS 111** if eczema becomes **weepy, crusty or very painful**, or you feel unwell. It may be infected.

Get help straight away if eczema suddenly becomes very painful with **small, punched-out sores or blisters** and a high temperature. This can be **eczema herpeticum**, a serious infection.
{% /callout %}

Read about [eczema treatment](/conditions/skin/eczema/treatment/).
